VeridianOS: dbus_message_iter_recurse() checks for a container

Real bug (prepared for upstream, gitlab.freedesktop.org/dbus/dbus):
calling dbus_message_iter_recurse() when the iterator is not on a
container -- easy to reach from an application that recurses without
checking dbus_message_iter_get_arg_type(), e.g. when a peer sends an
unexpected signature -- went into _dbus_type_reader_recurse(), whose
_dbus_assert_not_reached() is empty without assertions, and crashed
through a NULL reader class with no diagnostic (0001 turns that into an
explicit abort). The public entry point now checks the iterator type
and the current argument type like the other dbus_message_iter_*()
functions, so the usual "arguments to dbus_message_iter_recurse() were
incorrect" check failure is reported instead. Valid calls are
unaffected.

--- a/dbus/dbus-message.c
+++ b/dbus/dbus-message.c
@@ -2272,6 +2272,8 @@
 
   _dbus_return_if_fail (_dbus_message_iter_check (real));
   _dbus_return_if_fail (sub != NULL);
+  _dbus_return_if_fail (real->iter_type == DBUS_MESSAGE_ITER_TYPE_READER);
+  _dbus_return_if_fail (dbus_type_is_container (_dbus_type_reader_get_current_type (&real->u.reader)));
 
   *real_sub = *real;
   _dbus_type_reader_recurse (&real->u.reader, &real_sub->u.reader);
